        "content": "{{true and funny|source=NBC News obituary; BBC News obituary; Associated Press; Washington Post; Variety}}\n{{Infobox\n| title = Eric Dane\n| born = 9 November 1972, San Francisco\n| died = afternoon of 19 February 2026\n| age = 53\n| cause = ALS\n| diagnosed = 2024\n| early symptom = weakness in the right hand\n| role = Dr Mark Sloan on Grey's Anatomy\n| years = 2006-2012\n| nickname = McSteamy\n| scripted injury = plane crash at the end of season 8\n| scripted death = season 9 premiere\n| later role = Cal Jacobs on Euphoria\n| board = Target ALS, December 2025\n| campaign = helped a drive pass $500,000\n}}\n\n'''Eric Dane''' was born in San Francisco on 9 November 1972.<ref name=\"ap\">Associated Press. https://apnews.com/article/eric-dane-dead-a2eaf3916d047c038ac1121af9a99f17</ref> The Washington Post's obituary carries the same birthplace.<ref name=\"wapo\">Washington Post. https://www.washingtonpost.com/obituaries/2026/02/20/eric-dane-greys-anatomy-euphoria-death/</ref> Variety does too.<ref name=\"variety\">Variety. https://variety.com/2026/tv/news/eric-dane-dead-mcsteamy-greys-anatomy-euphoria-1236667968/</ref>\n\n== Sloan ==\nHe played Dr Mark Sloan.<ref name=\"nbc\">NBC News. https://www.nbcnews.com/news/obituaries/eric-dane-als-rcna259841</ref> The show was ''Grey's Anatomy''.<ref name=\"nbc\" /> The years on that series were 2006 to 2012.<ref name=\"nbc\" /> Viewers used the nickname McSteamy.<ref name=\"variety\" /> A plane crash at the end of season 8 left the character fatally injured.<ref name=\"nbc\" /> The on-screen death was the season 9 premiere.<ref name=\"nbc\" /> That crash is a plot point in the series.<ref name=\"nbc\" /> He also played Cal Jacobs.<ref name=\"bbc\">BBC News. https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c78j01n775ko</ref> The series for that part was ''Euphoria''.<ref name=\"bbc\" />\n\n== The afternoon ==\nHe died on the afternoon of Thursday 19 February 2026.<ref name=\"ap\" /> The age was 53.<ref name=\"wapo\" /> The cause was ALS.<ref name=\"variety\" /> The BBC reported the death.<ref name=\"bbc\" /> His family said he passed on Thursday afternoon following a courageous battle with ALS.<ref name=\"bbc\" /> The family's verb in that sentence is \"passed.\"<ref name=\"bbc\" />\n\n== The letters ==\nNBC reported a diagnosis in 2024.<ref name=\"nbc\" /> The symptom named there was weakness in his right hand.<ref name=\"nbc\" /> He told Diane Sawyer, \"I will never forget those three letters.\"<ref name=\"nbc\" /> ''People'', via that NBC obituary, carried a line dated 10 April 2025.<ref name=\"nbc\" /> He said he was grateful to have his loving family by his side as they navigate this next chapter.<ref name=\"nbc\" /> A video in September 2025 used a different line.<ref name=\"bbc\" /> He described himself as an actor, a father, and now a person living with ALS.<ref name=\"bbc\" /> I AM ALS, via NBC, called him a fierce advocate.<ref name=\"nbc\" />\n\n== The board ==\nIn December 2025 he joined the board of Target ALS.<ref name=\"bbc\" /> He helped a campaign pass $500,000.<ref name=\"bbc\" /> He told ''Time'', \"I'm trying to save my life.\"<ref name=\"bbc\" />\n\n== Where each line sits ==\nThe fatal injury belongs to the end of season 8.<ref name=\"nbc\" /> The character's death on the show is the next premiere.<ref name=\"nbc\" /> McSteamy is the nickname for Sloan.<ref name=\"variety\" /> The nickname belongs to that doctor.<ref name=\"variety\" /> ''Euphoria'' is a separate series from ''Grey's Anatomy''.<ref name=\"bbc\" /> Diane Sawyer is the interviewer on the line about the letters.<ref name=\"nbc\" /> The diagnosis NBC reported is the context for those letters.<ref name=\"nbc\" /> ''People'' is the magazine quoted for the April statement.<ref name=\"nbc\" /> His words there were, \"grateful to have my loving family by my side as we navigate this next chapter.\"<ref name=\"nbc\" /> The September recording is a video.<ref name=\"bbc\" /> The video is a separate recording from the April statement.<ref name=\"bbc\" /> In the video he named himself an actor.<ref name=\"bbc\" /> He named himself a father.<ref name=\"bbc\" /> He named himself a person living with ALS.<ref name=\"bbc\" /> Target ALS is the board.<ref name=\"bbc\" /> I AM ALS is the other organization in the obituaries.<ref name=\"nbc\" /> The fierce-advocate line is the I AM ALS line.<ref name=\"nbc\" /> NBC is the outlet that carried it.<ref name=\"nbc\" /> The ''Time'' sentence is the one about saving his life.<ref name=\"bbc\" /> The Washington Post obituary is dated 20 February 2026.<ref name=\"wapo\" /> That Post piece confirms the San Francisco birth.<ref name=\"wapo\" /> It confirms the death date.<ref name=\"wapo\" /> It confirms ALS.<ref name=\"wapo\" /> Variety confirms the birthplace in its own report.<ref name=\"variety\" /> The Associated Press confirms the illness in its own report.<ref name=\"ap\" /> The family's phrase for the illness is \"a courageous battle.\"<ref name=\"bbc\" />\n\nThe character died in a plane crash written for season 8. In December 2025 he joined a board chasing $500,000 and told Time he was trying to save his life.\n\nThe ''Guardian'' obituary gives the same dates, 9 November 1972 and 19 February 2026, and says Mark Sloan dies in hospital after the plane crash.<ref name=\"guardiane\">Anthony Hayward, \"Eric Dane obituary\", ''The Guardian'', 20 February 2026. https://www.theguardian.com/tv-and-radio/2026/feb/20/eric-dane-obituary</ref>\n== References ==\n{{reflist}}\n\n[[Category:Actors]]\n[[Category:Television]]\n[[Category:True but improbable]]\n"
