        "comment": "expanded from the WBD release, Variety, the Television Academy, the Los Angeles Times, the Hollywood Reporter, and Deadline",
        "content": "{{true and funny|source=Warner Bros. Discovery, 12 December 2024; Variety on the format and Variety, 11 April 2025; Television Academy; Los Angeles Times, 14 September 2026; Hollywood Reporter, 14 September 2026; Deadline, 14 September 2026}}\n{{Infobox\n| title = The Pitt\n| type = Max Original\n| companies = John Wells Productions and Warner Bros. Television\n| creator = R. Scott Gemmill\n| star = Noah Wyle as Dr. Michael \"Robby\" Robinavitch\n| hospital = Pittsburgh Trauma Medical Hospital\n| role there = chief attendant\n| episodes = 15\n| debut = two episodes, Thursday 9 January\n| finale = 10 April\n| shift on the release = 15 hours, one hour an episode\n| shift in the Times = 12 hours, one hour an episode\n| producers = R. Scott Gemmill, Noah Wyle, and John Wells\n| viewers = first 14 episodes averaged 10 million\n| premiere viewers = episode 1 crossed 16.2 million\n| emmys = 11, Television Academy\n| 2026 night = seven, Hollywood Reporter\n}}\n\n'''The Pitt''' is a Max Original from John Wells Productions and Warner Bros. Television, created by R. Scott Gemmill, starring Noah Wyle as Dr. Michael \"Robby\" Robinavitch.<ref name=\"wbd\">Warner Bros. Discovery press release, 12 December 2024. https://press.wbd.com/us/media-release/hbo-max/max-original-drama-series-pitt-debuts-january-9</ref> Variety's account is that it is not an ER revival, that Wyle did not reprise John Carter, and that he, John Wells, and Gemmill made a fresh take. Reviving ER has been legally disputed by the estate of series co-creator Michael Crichton.<ref name=\"format\">Variety, on The Pitt, Noah Wyle, John Wells, and the differences from ER. https://variety.com/2025/tv/news/the-pitt-noah-wyle-john-wells-er-differences-real-time-format-1236283401/</ref> The Television Academy lists 11 Emmys, including 2026 wins for Outstanding Drama Series and for Outstanding Lead Actor in a Drama Series for Noah Wyle as Dr. Robby.<ref name=\"academy\">Television Academy, The Pitt. https://www.televisionacademy.com/shows/the-pitt</ref>\n\n== The release ==\nThe 12 December 2024 release says two episodes debuted on Thursday 9 January. Each episode follows an hour of Dr. Robby's 15-hour shift as chief attendant at Pittsburgh Trauma Medical Hospital. There are 15 episodes. The finale is 10 April.<ref name=\"wbd\" /> Executive producers include Gemmill, Wyle, and John Wells, all credited \"with ER.\"<ref name=\"wbd\" /> The credit is on the release. The sentence that the series is not a revival of ER is in Variety.<ref name=\"wbd\" /><ref name=\"format\" /> Variety says the season plays out over one shift, each of the 15 episodes representing a single hour.<ref name=\"format\" /> The Los Angeles Times, on 14 September 2026, describes each hour of a 12-hour shift.<ref name=\"latimes\">Los Angeles Times, 14 September 2026. https://www.latimes.com/entertainment-arts/awards/story/2026-09-14/2026-emmys-noah-wyle-the-pitt-lead-actor-drama</ref> Wyle's line in the Variety story is: \"If you ever want to revisit this world in any way and talk about what's going on and you need a mouthpiece for that, I'm your man.\"<ref name=\"format\" />\n\n== The count of viewers ==\nVariety's count on 11 April 2025 stops at the first 14 episodes. Those 14 averaged 10 million viewers. Episode 1 crossed 16.2 million.<ref name=\"ratings\">Variety, 11 April 2025. https://variety.com/2025/tv/news/the-pitt-ratings-viewers-1236367516/</ref> The release sets a finale on 10 April. Variety's audience story is dated the next day.<ref name=\"wbd\" /><ref name=\"ratings\" />\n\n== Two Septembers in 2026 ==\nOn 14 September 2026 the Los Angeles Times reported that Wyle won lead actor in a drama for the \"second year in a row,\" and that the show also won drama series that night. The Times says season 2 premiered in January, and that season 3 is slated to premiere in January 2027.<ref name=\"latimes\" /> The Hollywood Reporter, the same day, called the drama-series win the \"second consecutive\" Outstanding Drama Series Emmy, counted seven Emmys in total that night, and said the show is set to return in January 2027.<ref name=\"thr\">Hollywood Reporter, 14 September 2026. https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/tv/tv-news/2026-emmys-best-drama-series-the-pitt-wins-1236700918/</ref> The Academy page is the cumulative list. The Reporter's seven is that night's haul. The Academy's 11 is the page's total. The Times' phrase is the lead-actor win. The Reporter's phrase is the drama series.<ref name=\"academy\" /><ref name=\"latimes\" /><ref name=\"thr\" /> Deadline, the same night, quoted Wyle: \"I'm a Hollywood kid. I was raised on a television.\"<ref name=\"deadline\">Deadline, 14 September 2026. https://deadline.com/2026/09/noah-wyle-emmy-win-2026-the-pitt-lead-drama-actor-repeat-1237102890/</ref>\n\n== What the pages will not share ==\nVariety's format story says one shift and one hour an episode, and does not pick up the Times figure.<ref name=\"format\" /><ref name=\"latimes\" /> The January premiere of season 2, in the Times, has no day attached. Season 3, in the Times, and the return, in the Reporter, are both January 2027.<ref name=\"latimes\" /><ref name=\"thr\" /> The legal sentence stays in Variety. The estate of Michael Crichton has disputed reviving ER. The series is described there as a fresh take, and Wyle is described there as not playing John Carter again.<ref name=\"format\" />\n\nThey could not call it ER. The Academy called it the outstanding drama, two years running.\n\n== References ==\n{{reflist}}\n\n[[Category:Television]]\n[[Category:True but improbable]]\n"
