        "daysAgo": 4,
        "comment": "expanded from the Guardian, the Academy speech database, the Hollywood Reporter, Variety, and the two BBC pages",
        "content": "{{true and funny|source=Guardian interview, 25 December 2010; Academy Awards Acceptance Speech Database, 85th Oscars; Hollywood Reporter, 25 March 2024; Variety, 25 March 2024; BBC Films, 5 March 2007; BBC, 25 February 2013}}\n{{Infobox\n| title = Anne Hathaway\n| born = 12th November, 1982\n| named for = Shakespeare's wife, as the Guardian states\n| cottage = she said she had never seen it\n| early role = The Princess Diaries\n| oscar = supporting actress, Les Misérables, 85th Oscars\n| date = 24 February 2013\n| theatre = Dolby Theatre\n| presenter = Christopher Plummer\n| role = Fantine\n| opened = It came true\n| backlash = Hathahate, Hollywood Reporter, 25 March 2024\n| nolan role = Dr. Amelia Brand, NASA scientist, Interstellar (2014)\n| opposite = Matthew McConaughey\n}}\n\n'''Anne Hathaway''' was born on 12th November, 1982, which is the date of birth on the BBC Films page of 5 March 2007.<ref name=\"bbcfilms\">BBC Films, 5 March 2007. https://www.bbc.com/films/2007/03/05/anne_hathaway_2007_becoming_jane_interview.shtml</ref> The Guardian, in an interview of 25 December 2010 carried at a URL dated 26 December, states that she was named after Shakespeare's wife. She said she had never seen Anne Hathaway's cottage. She made her name in ''The Princess Diaries''.<ref name=\"guardian\">Guardian, interview dated 25 December 2010. https://www.theguardian.com/film/2010/dec/26/anne-hathaway-interview-love-other-drugs</ref> Asked if she always dreamt of being a princess, she said: \"No, I dreamt of being an actress! That was a role I played!\"<ref name=\"guardian\" />\n\n== The cottage and the diary ==\nThe name is Shakespeare's wife. The cottage is a place she said she had not seen. ''The Princess Diaries'' is the film the interview credits with making her name.<ref name=\"guardian\" /> The answer refuses the dream of being a princess and calls the part a role.<ref name=\"guardian\" /> The birth date is not in that interview's sentences as used here. It is on the BBC Films page, printed as 12th November, 1982.<ref name=\"bbcfilms\" />\n\n== Two words ==\nThe Academy Awards Acceptance Speech Database records the supporting-actress award for ''Les Misérables'' at the 85th Oscars, on 24 February 2013, at the Dolby Theatre. Christopher Plummer presented. She played Fantine. The speech opened \"It came true.\"<ref name=\"speech\">Academy Awards Acceptance Speech Database, 85th Oscars, supporting actress. https://aaspeechesdb.oscars.org/link/085-4/</ref> The database says the speech ends hoping the misfortunes of Fantine will only be found in stories and nevermore in real life.<ref name=\"speech\" /> The BBC, on 25 February 2013, reported that she won best supporting actress for Fantine and that she said \"It came true.\"<ref name=\"bbcnews\">BBC, 25 February 2013. https://www.bbc.com/news/entertainment-arts-21570004</ref> The database adds the theatre, the presenter, the date of the ceremony, and the hope at the end. The BBC story is dated the day after the ceremony.<ref name=\"speech\" /><ref name=\"bbcnews\" /> The database names the presenter as Christopher Plummer. The Dolby Theatre is the room. Fantine is the role. Supporting actress is the category. The 85th Oscars is the ceremony number.<ref name=\"speech\" />\n\n== The word the Reporter used ==\nOn 25 March 2024 the Hollywood Reporter used \"Hathahate\" for the backlash after that win and after the 2013 Oscars she co-hosted.<ref name=\"thr\">Hollywood Reporter, 25 March 2024. https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-news/anne-hathaway-hathahate-christopher-nolan-1235859456/</ref> Variety, the same day, quoted her: \"A lot of people wouldn't give me roles because they were so concerned about how toxic my identity had become online.\"<ref name=\"variety\">Variety, 25 March 2024. https://variety.com/2024/film/news/anne-hathaway-christopher-nolan-saved-career-toxic-oscar-backlash-1235950940/</ref> The sentence is about roles withheld because of an identity online.<ref name=\"variety\" />\n\n== The scientist ==\nVariety reports that Christopher Nolan cast her as the NASA scientist Dr. Amelia Brand in ''Interstellar'' (2014), opposite Matthew McConaughey. Her line on that casting is: \"I had an angel in Christopher Nolan, who did not care about that and gave me one of the most beautiful roles I've had in one of the best films that I've been a part of.\"<ref name=\"variety\" /> The \"that\" in \"did not care about that\" is, in the Variety story, the online identity she had just described.<ref name=\"variety\" /> The Reporter's word for the backlash is Hathahate. Variety's quotation is the hiring sentence and the Nolan sentence. They ran on the same date.<ref name=\"thr\" /><ref name=\"variety\" />\n\nThe speech began \"It came true.\" The next roles did not, until a director who did not care.\n\n== References ==\n{{reflist}}\n\n[[Category:People]]\n[[Category:True but improbable]]\n"
