        "comment": "expanded from Bell Media, Variety, the Hollywood Reporter, and Deadline",
        "content": "{{true and funny|source=Bell Media, 9 October 2025; Variety, 12 December 2025 and 9 February 2026; Hollywood Reporter, 25 November 2025 and 28 January 2026; Deadline, 9 February 2026}}\n{{Infobox\n| title = Heated Rivalry\n| type = Crave original\n| creator = Jacob Tierney\n| source = Rachel Reid, Game Changers, six books\n| first book = published 2019, the book the series is named after\n| episodes = six, one hour each\n| premiere = two episodes, Friday 28 November 2025\n| finale = 26 December 2025\n| hollander = Hudson Williams as Shane Hollander\n| rozanov = Connor Storrie as Ilya Rozanov\n| sport = rival Major League Hockey players\n| consulting = Rachel Reid, consulting producer\n| us rights = HBO Max, acquired nine days before the premiere\n| season 2 = renewed; HBO Max licenses, does not produce\n| january average = 9 million U.S. viewers per episode, 28 January 2026\n| february average = 10.6 million U.S. viewers per episode, 9 February 2026\n}}\n\n'''Heated Rivalry''' is a Crave original created by Jacob Tierney and adapted from Rachel Reid's six-book Harlequin series ''Game Changers''. Bell Media's release of 9 October 2025 says it is six one-hour episodes, that two episodes premiered on Friday 28 November 2025, that it ran weekly after that, and that the finale was 26 December 2025.<ref name=\"bell\">Bell Media, 9 October 2025. https://www.bellmedia.ca/the-lede/press/crave-breaks-the-ice-with-trailer-and-debut-date-for-its-new-original-series-heated-rivalry-premiering-november-28/</ref> Variety, on 9 February 2026, says the show is named after the first ''Game Changers'' book, published in 2019.<ref name=\"varfeb\">Variety, 9 February 2026. https://variety.com/2026/tv/news/heated-rivalry-ratings-viewers-1236657375/</ref> Reid is a consulting producer.<ref name=\"bell\" />\n\n== The players ==\nHudson Williams plays Shane Hollander. Connor Storrie plays Ilya Rozanov. Bell Media calls them rival Major League Hockey players.<ref name=\"bell\" /> The same release casts François Arnaud as Scott Hunter, Robbie G.K. as Kip Grady, Christina Chang and Dylan Walsh as Yuna and David Hollander, Sophie Nélisse as Rose Landry, and Ksenia Daniela Kharlamova as Svetlana.<ref name=\"bell\" /> The hockey league in that sentence is Major League Hockey, the name the release prints.<ref name=\"bell\" />\n\n== The dates on the release ==\nVariety's 9 February 2026 story uses that same finale date for the episode it compares with premiere week.<ref name=\"varfeb\" /> Jacob Tierney, in the Hollywood Reporter of 25 November 2025, said: \"Come for the smuttiness, stay for the romance.\"<ref name=\"thrnov\">Hollywood Reporter, 25 November 2025. https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/tv/tv-features/heated-rivalry-social-media-popularity-crave-hbo-max-1236432001/</ref> The sentence is dated three days before the premiere on the Bell Media release. It names smuttiness and romance. It does not name a viewer average.<ref name=\"thrnov\" /><ref name=\"bell\" />\n\n== Who makes it ==\nVariety, on 12 December 2025, says the series also streams on HBO Max in the United States, that Crave produces it, that it was renewed for season 2, and that HBO Max will continue to license the series for its second season without producing.<ref name=\"vardec\">Variety, 12 December 2025. https://variety.com/2025/tv/news/heated-rivalry-renewed-season-2-hbo-max-crave-1236605666/</ref> The Hollywood Reporter, on 28 January 2026, says HBO Max acquired the U.S. rights just nine days before the series premiered.<ref name=\"thrjan\">Hollywood Reporter, 28 January 2026. https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/tv/tv-news/heated-rivalry-ratings-9-million-viewers-hbo-max-1236487815/</ref> The nine days are the Reporter's count, printed on 28 January 2026.<ref name=\"thrjan\" /> The December Variety story, written during the run, already has HBO Max carrying the series in the United States and already has the season-2 license without production.<ref name=\"vardec\" />\n\n== Nine million, then 10.6 million ==\nTHR's 28 January 2026 figure is an average of 9 million U.S. viewers per episode.<ref name=\"thrjan\" /> Variety, on 9 February 2026, says WBD told Variety the series reached an average of 10.6 million U.S. viewers per episode.<ref name=\"varfeb\" /> Deadline, on 9 February 2026, says it surpassed 10.6 million U.S. viewers.<ref name=\"deadline\">Deadline, 9 February 2026. https://deadline.com/2026/02/heated-rivalry-season-1-finale-viewership-hbo-max-1236713589/</ref> The January figure and the February figures are different dates. Variety states an average WBD gave. Deadline states a number the series had surpassed.<ref name=\"thrjan\" /><ref name=\"varfeb\" /><ref name=\"deadline\" />\n\nVariety's 9 February story says the finale was up more than 300 percent from premiere week, that the audience had more than doubled since the finale, and that WBD called it the most-watched scripted title acquired from another platform in HBO Max history.<ref name=\"varfeb\" /> Deadline's story of the same day says the finale delivered four times the audience of the premiere.<ref name=\"deadline\" /> More than 300 percent from premiere week is Variety's comparison. Four times the premiere is Deadline's comparison. More than doubled since the finale is Variety's other comparison. None is used here to correct the others.<ref name=\"varfeb\" /><ref name=\"deadline\" />\n\nHBO Max bought the American rights nine days before the premiere and did not produce the second season. The viewers were theirs anyway.\n\n== References ==\n{{reflist}}\n\n[[Category:Television]]\n[[Category:True but improbable]]\n"
