        "content": "{{true and funny|source=Netflix Tudum, 26 August 2025; CBC, 27 August 2025; WLRN, 17 September 2025; Variety, 15 September 2025; ABC, 26 August 2025; Washington Post, 13 August 2025}}\n{{Infobox\n| title = KPop Demon Hunters\n| directors = Maggie Kang and Chris Appelhans\n| production = Sony Pictures Animation, with Netflix\n| premiered = 20 June 2025, Netflix\n| views = more than 236 million\n| streams = more than 3 billion, global, soundtrack\n| billboard 200 = No. 1 in its 12th week, 128,000 equivalent units\n| hot 100 = How It's Done, Your Idol, Soda Pop, and Golden\n| number one = Golden\n| group = Huntr/x\n| sing-along = 1,750 theatres in Canada and the United States\n| screen estimate = $18 million to $20 million\n}}\n\n'''KPop Demon Hunters''' is a Netflix original directed by Maggie Kang and Chris Appelhans and produced with Sony Pictures Animation.<ref name=\"tudum\">Netflix Tudum, 26 August 2025. https://www.netflix.com/tudum/articles/kpop-demon-hunters-most-popular-netflix-film</ref> CBC dates the premiere to 20 June 2025.<ref name=\"cbc\">CBC, 27 August 2025. https://www.cbc.ca/news/entertainment/kpop-demon-hunters-netflix-streaming-record-1.7618350</ref> Tudum, on 26 August 2025, calls it the most popular Netflix film to date, with more than 236 million views, and says the soundtrack was the first with four simultaneous Billboard Hot 100 top 10s.<ref name=\"tudum\" />\n\n== What CBC says happens ==\nCBC's account of the film: the K-pop trio Huntr/x, Rumi, Mira, and Zoey, use music to fight soul-eating demons held back by a barrier called the Honmoon, until the demon band the Saja Boys overtakes them, while Rumi hides demon ancestry.<ref name=\"cbc\" /> The same CBC story gives 236 million views as of Tuesday, surpassing ''Red Notice'', and describes sing-along screenings in 1,750 theatres in Canada and the United States that weekend, estimated at $18 million to $20 million.<ref name=\"cbc\" /> The view count is \"as of Tuesday\" in a story dated 27 August 2025. The theatre count and the estimate belong to that weekend's sing-alongs, not to the premiere.<ref name=\"cbc\" />\n\n== 236 million, three ways ==\nABC, on 26 August 2025, says Netflix said 236 million views, the most popular film of all time on its English-films list.<ref name=\"abc\">ABC News, 26 August 2025. https://abcnews.com/GMA/Culture/golden-kpop-demon-hunters-hits-1-billboard-surpasses/story?id=124560076</ref> Tudum's phrase is the whole Netflix film list, with a \"more than.\" ABC's phrase is the English-films list. CBC's phrase is the same number, tied to a Tuesday and to passing ''Red Notice''.<ref name=\"tudum\" /><ref name=\"abc\" /><ref name=\"cbc\" /> Tudum also says the soundtrack had more than 3 billion global streams, and that it was No. 2 on the Billboard 200 for the week of 23 August.<ref name=\"tudum\" /> The streams figure is Tudum's. It is not the unit count Variety later uses for one chart week.<ref name=\"tudum\" />\n\n== Four titles ==\nCBC names the four Hot 100 titles: \"How It's Done,\" \"Your Idol,\" \"Soda Pop,\" and \"Golden,\" with Golden at No. 1. CBC says Huntr/x was the first K-pop girl group to top the Hot 100.<ref name=\"cbc\" /> The Washington Post, on 13 August 2025, says Golden was the first K-pop girl group in Billboard history to claim the No. 1 spot.<ref name=\"post\">Washington Post, 13 August 2025. https://www.washingtonpost.com/entertainment/music/2025/08/13/kpop-demon-hunters-golden-billboard-one/</ref> CBC's first is the group, Huntr/x. The Post's first is the song, Golden.<ref name=\"cbc\" /><ref name=\"post\" /> Tudum's chart sentence is about the soundtrack: the first with four songs in the Hot 100 top 10 at the same time.<ref name=\"tudum\" />\n\n== The twelfth week ==\nVariety, on 15 September 2025, says the soundtrack reached No. 1 on the Billboard 200, and that Golden was in its fifth week at No. 1 on the Hot 100.<ref name=\"variety\">Variety, 15 September 2025. https://variety.com/2025/music/news/kpop-demon-hunters-soundtrack-number-one-golden-charts-1236519478/</ref> Variety's unit count for that No. 1 week is 128,000 equivalent units.<ref name=\"variety\" /> WLRN, in a piece by Stephen Thompson dated 17 September 2025, says the soundtrack hit No. 1 on the Billboard 200 in its 12th week, that Golden was No. 1 for a fifth nonconsecutive week, and that four soundtrack songs were still in the Hot 100 top 10.<ref name=\"wlrn\">WLRN, Stephen Thompson, 17 September 2025. https://www.wlrn.org/2025-09-17/kpop-demon-hunters-finally-tops-the-albums-chart</ref> Variety says Golden's fifth week at No. 1. WLRN says a fifth \"nonconsecutive\" week. The word is WLRN's. The unit count is Variety's. Tudum's No. 2, for the week of 23 August, is earlier than both.<ref name=\"variety\" /><ref name=\"wlrn\" /><ref name=\"tudum\" />\n\nFour songs sat in the Hot 100 top ten at once, from a film about a girl group that fights demons. Golden was the one at number one.\n\n== References ==\n{{reflist}}\n\n[[Category:Films]]\n[[Category:True but improbable]]\n"
