        "comment": "expanded from the accession page, three BBC reports, the 70-facts page, the life-and-reign page, and the 2003 coronation note",
        "content": "{{true and funny|source=royal.uk accession and coronation; BBC, 9 September 2015; BBC, 8 September 2022; BBC, 8 September 2023; royal.uk 70 facts; royal.uk life and reign; royal.uk 50 coronation facts}}\n{{Infobox\n| title = Elizabeth II\n| acceded = 6 February 1952\n| where the news reached her = Kenya\n| predecessor = George VI\n| crowned = 2 June 1953, Westminster Abbey\n| crowned by = Archbishop Geoffrey Fisher\n| coronation broadcast = first televised coronation, at her request\n| guests = 8,251\n| died = afternoon of 8 September 2022, Balmoral\n| age at death = 96\n| prime ministers = 15, Churchill through Liz Truss\n| last prime minister appointed = Liz Truss, who served 49 days\n| age at accession = just 25\n| wartime service = Auxiliary Territorial Service, 1945, as a subaltern\n| dogs = more than 30 corgis and dorgis, most descended from Susan\n}}\n\n'''Elizabeth II''' acceded on 6 February 1952, when George VI died.<ref name=\"acc\">royal.uk, accession and coronation. https://www.royal.uk/queen-elizabeth-iis-accession-and-coronation</ref> News of his death reached her in Kenya.<ref name=\"acc\" /> A 2003 royal.uk note on the coronation says she succeeded on that February date in Kenya.<ref name=\"fifty\">royal.uk, 50 facts about the coronation, a 2003 press note. https://www.royal.uk/50-facts-about-queen-elizabeth-iis-coronation</ref> The same note calls her the first Sovereign in over 200 years to accede while abroad.<ref name=\"fifty\" />\n\n== The abbey ==\nShe was crowned at Westminster Abbey on 2 June 1953.<ref name=\"acc\" /> The archbishop was Geoffrey Fisher.<ref name=\"acc\" /> The coronation was televised at her request, and the accession page calls it the first televised coronation.<ref name=\"acc\" /> About 27 million people in Britain watched, on that page.<ref name=\"acc\" /> The 2003 note says the coronation was watched by 27 million in the UK.<ref name=\"fifty\" /> The guest count on the 2003 note is 8,251.<ref name=\"fifty\" /> The life-and-reign page says she acceded aged just 25.<ref name=\"life\">royal.uk, life and reign. https://www.royal.uk/queen-elizabeth-iis-life-and-reign</ref>\n\n== The sentence about the record ==\nOn 9 September 2015, at 17:30 BST, the BBC calculated the reign at 23,226 days, 16 hours and about 30 minutes.<ref name=\"bbc2015\">BBC, 9 September 2015. https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-34177107</ref> She said the title was \"not one to which I have ever aspired.\"<ref name=\"bbc2015\" />\n\n== Fifteen prime ministers ==\nShe died peacefully on the afternoon of 8 September 2022 at Balmoral, aged 96.<ref name=\"bbc2022\">BBC, 8 September 2022. https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-61585886</ref> The BBC death story says the reign spanned 15 prime ministers, from Winston Churchill through Liz Truss.<ref name=\"bbc2022\" /> Truss had been appointed the Tuesday before the death.<ref name=\"bbc2022\" /> The life-and-reign page also says she was served by 15 UK prime ministers, beginning with Churchill in 1952.<ref name=\"life\" /> This article uses that count from those two pages.<ref name=\"bbc2022\" /><ref name=\"life\" /> It does not print a smaller figure that has been reported of the 70-facts page.<ref name=\"life\" /><ref name=\"facts\">royal.uk, 70 facts for the Platinum Jubilee, 6 May 2022. https://www.royal.uk/70-facts-mark-queens-platinum-jubilee</ref>\n\nA BBC story of 8 September 2023 says Truss served 49 days.<ref name=\"bbc2023\">BBC, 8 September 2023. https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-england-norfolk-66744659</ref> The same story calls her the last prime minister Elizabeth II appointed.<ref name=\"bbc2023\" /> The life-and-reign page says she welcomed over 110 presidents and prime ministers.<ref name=\"life\" /> The 70-facts page names Winston Churchill as the first prime minister.<ref name=\"facts\" />\n\n== Susan ==\nIn 1945 she joined the Auxiliary Territorial Service as a subaltern.<ref name=\"facts\" /> The 70-facts page calls her the first female member of the royal family to serve full-time.<ref name=\"facts\" /> She learned to drive and maintain vehicles.<ref name=\"facts\" /> The same page says she owned more than 30 corgis and dorgis.<ref name=\"facts\" /> Most of them descended from Susan.<ref name=\"facts\" /> Susan was given to her on her 18th birthday in 1944.<ref name=\"facts\" />\n\nHer last prime minister lasted 49 days. The corgis went back to a dog named Susan, a gift on her 18th birthday. She said the record was not a title she had ever aspired to.\n\n== References ==\n{{reflist}}\n\n[[Category:Monarchs]]\n[[Category:True but improbable]]\n"
