        "content": "{{true and funny|source=royal.uk; Historic Royal Palaces, Kensington; London Museum; National Portrait Gallery; Westminster Abbey}}\n{{Infobox\n| title = Queen Victoria\n| born = 24 May 1819, Kensington Palace\n| accession on the palace page = 20 June 1837\n| npg timing = shortly after her eighteenth birthday, in July 1837\n| crowned = 28 June 1838, aged 19\n| married = 10 February 1840, Chapel Royal, St James's Palace\n| dress = white Spitalfields satin and lace\n| height = 5 ft 1 in\n| empress of India = 1877, Royal Titles Act\n| children = nine, between 1840 and 1857\n| albert died = 14 December 1861, aged 42\n| died = 22 January 1901, Osborne House, Isle of Wight\n| reign on the palace page = 63 years, seven months and two days\n| jubilees = Golden 1887, Diamond 1897\n}}\n\n'''Queen Victoria''' was born at Kensington Palace on 24 May 1819.<ref name=\"royal\">royal.uk. https://www.royal.uk/encyclopedia/victoria-r-1837-1901</ref> Westminster Abbey's page gives the same birthplace and the same day.<ref name=\"abbey\">Westminster Abbey. https://www.westminster-abbey.org/abbey-commemorations/royals/victoria/</ref> Royal.uk says she became queen at 18, on the death of William IV, in 1837.<ref name=\"royal\" />\n\n== June, and a July sentence ==\nHistoric Royal Palaces dates the accession to 20 June 1837.<ref name=\"hrp\">Historic Royal Palaces, Kensington Palace. https://www.hrp.org.uk/kensington-palace/history-and-stories/queen-victoria/</ref> The National Portrait Gallery says she succeeded shortly after her eighteenth birthday, in July 1837.<ref name=\"npg\">National Portrait Gallery. https://www.npg.org.uk/collections/search/person/mp04634/queen-victoria</ref> The June date and the July sentence are both printed here.<ref name=\"hrp\" /><ref name=\"npg\" /> This article does not correct one of them with the other.<ref name=\"hrp\" /><ref name=\"npg\" />\n\nThe palace page gives the reign as 63 years, seven months and two days.<ref name=\"hrp\" /> The National Portrait Gallery describes a reign of 63 years.<ref name=\"npg\" /> The Gallery's jubilees are the Golden Jubilee of 1887 and the Diamond Jubilee of 1897.<ref name=\"npg\" />\n\nWestminster Abbey says she was crowned on 28 June 1838, aged 19.<ref name=\"abbey\" /> The palace page calls her physically tiny, at 5 ft 1 in.<ref name=\"hrp\" /> The Duke of Wellington's line on that page is: \"she not only filled her chair, she filled the room.\"<ref name=\"hrp\" />\n\n== She asked ==\nThe London Museum says nobody could propose to a reigning monarch, so she proposed to Albert in 1839.<ref name=\"museum\">London Museum. https://www.londonmuseum.org.uk/collections/london-stories/marriage-queen-victoria-prince-albert/</ref> Her words on that page are: \"It would make me too happy if he would consent to what I wished (to marry me).\"<ref name=\"museum\" /> The London Museum prints her proposal in her own words.\n\nThey married on 10 February 1840.<ref name=\"museum\" /> The place was the Chapel Royal at St James's Palace.<ref name=\"museum\" /> Westminster Abbey gives the same day and the same chapel.<ref name=\"abbey\" /> The dress was white Spitalfields satin and lace.<ref name=\"museum\" />\n\n== Black ==\nRoyal.uk says Albert died on 14 December 1861, aged 42.<ref name=\"royal\" /> She wore black for the rest of her reign.<ref name=\"royal\" /> The London Museum also records the death and the black.<ref name=\"museum\" /> Nine children were born between 1840 and 1857, on the royal.uk page.<ref name=\"royal\" /> In 1877 she became Empress of India under the Royal Titles Act.<ref name=\"royal\" />\n\nShe died at Osborne House on 22 January 1901.<ref name=\"royal\" /> Royal.uk places the house on the Isle of Wight.<ref name=\"royal\" /> Westminster Abbey also dates the death to 22 January 1901 at Osborne House.<ref name=\"abbey\" />\n\nWilliam IV's death is the occasion royal.uk gives for the accession.<ref name=\"royal\" /> The palace page is the one that dates the day in June.<ref name=\"hrp\" /> The Gallery's sentence puts the succession in July, shortly after the birthday.<ref name=\"npg\" /> The Abbey page is the one that dates the coronation and gives her age that day.<ref name=\"abbey\" /> Kensington's history is the page with the height and with Wellington.<ref name=\"hrp\" /> The London Museum is the page with the reason she had to ask, the wording of the ask, the cloth, and the chapel.<ref name=\"museum\" /> Royal.uk is the page with the children, the Royal Titles Act, Albert's death, and the black.<ref name=\"royal\" /> The Abbey page gives the marriage and the death without the island.<ref name=\"abbey\" /> The island is royal.uk's.<ref name=\"royal\" />\n\nShe proposed. The dress was white Spitalfields satin. After 14 December 1861 she wore black. Wellington had already said she filled the room.\n\nA separate page on the royal site says she was the first reigning monarch to use a train, and that she made that ride in 1842. It also says seven attempts were made on her life between 1840 and 1882.<ref name=\"royalvic2\">\"Queen Victoria\", The Royal Family. https://www.royal.uk/queen-victoria</ref>\n== References ==\n{{reflist}}\n\n[[Category:Monarchs]]\n[[Category:True but improbable]]\n"
