        "content": "{{true and funny|source=BBC Sport, 6 February 2026; BBC medal table; NBC Olympics; Guardian medal table, 22 February 2026; Guardian live blog, 22 February 2026}}\n{{Infobox\n| title = 2026 Winter Olympics\n| dates = 6 February to 22 February 2026\n| host = Milan-Cortina\n| opening sites = San Siro, Cortina, Livigno, Predazzo\n| cauldrons = two, one in Milan and one in Cortina\n| opened by = Kirsty Coventry, IOC president\n| sports = 16 winter sports\n| first = Norway\n| norway on the BBC table = 18 gold, 12 silver, 11 bronze, 41 total\n| second = United States\n| united states on the BBC table = 12 gold, 12 silver, 9 bronze, 33 total\n| cross-country sweep = Johannes Hoesflot Klaebo, 6-for-6 on NBC\n| guardian spelling = Johannes Høsflot Klæbo\n| ski-mountaineering = Oriol Cardona Coll, Spain\n| closed = Verona Arena, 22 February 2026\n}}\n\n'''The 2026 Winter Olympics''' ran from 6 February to 22 February in Milan-Cortina.<ref name=\"open\">BBC Sport, 6 February 2026. https://www.bbc.com/sport/articles/czejep5z51wo</ref> The opening was split across Milan's San Siro, Cortina, Livigno, and Predazzo.<ref name=\"open\" /> There were two Olympic cauldrons.<ref name=\"open\" /> One was in Milan.<ref name=\"open\" /> One was in Cortina.<ref name=\"open\" /> IOC president Kirsty Coventry declared the Games open.<ref name=\"open\" /> The BBC says that, as the first female IOC president, this was the first time a woman officially opened an Olympics.<ref name=\"open\" />\n\n== The table ==\nThe BBC medal table places Norway first, with 18 gold, 12 silver, 11 bronze, and 41 in all.<ref name=\"bbcmed\">BBC Sport medal table. https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/winter-olympics/italy-2026/medals</ref> The United States is second on that table, with 12 gold, 12 silver, 9 bronze, and 33 in all.<ref name=\"bbcmed\" /> NBC prints 18 golds and 41 medals for Norway.<ref name=\"nbc\">NBC Olympics. https://www.nbcolympics.com/news/norway-leaves-milan-cortina-olympics-record-medal-count-usa-second</ref> The Guardian's medal table of 22 February 2026 prints that same gold count and that same Norwegian total.<ref name=\"gmed\">Guardian medal table, 22 February 2026. https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/feb/22/winter-olympics-2026-medal-table-standings-norway</ref> The Guardian calls the gold count the most by a country in Winter Olympics history.<ref name=\"gmed\" /> Its second-place line is the United States, with 12 golds and 33 medals.<ref name=\"gmed\" />\n\nNBC says the Games held 16 winter sports.<ref name=\"nbc\" />\n\n== The skier, spelled twice ==\nNBC spells him Johannes Hoesflot Klaebo.<ref name=\"nbc\" /> That page says he went 6-for-6, sweeping the golds in his cross-country events.<ref name=\"nbc\" /> The Guardian spells him Johannes Høsflot Klæbo.<ref name=\"gmed\" /> That medal table says he accounted for six golds.<ref name=\"gmed\" />\n\nSki-mountaineering was on the programme for the first time, in NBC's account.<ref name=\"nbc\" /> Spaniard Oriol Cardona Coll won the gold.<ref name=\"nbc\" /> NBC calls it Spain's first Winter Olympic gold in 54 years.<ref name=\"nbc\" />\n\n== Verona ==\nThe Guardian's live blog of 22 February 2026 places the closing at the Verona Arena.<ref name=\"live\">Guardian live blog, 22 February 2026. https://www.theguardian.com/sport/live/2026/feb/22/winter-olympics-2026-gold-medals-bobsleigh-curling-canada-usa-ice-hockey-final-closing-ceremony-live</ref> The closing was held at the Verona Arena. The line it records is: \"I now declare the Milano-Cortina Winter Games closed.\"<ref name=\"live\" />\n\nThe BBC report that describes the opening is dated the day the Games began.<ref name=\"open\" /> Its host spelling is Milan-Cortina.<ref name=\"open\" /> The sentence recorded at the closing uses Milano-Cortina.<ref name=\"live\" /> San Siro is the Milan site in the split opening.<ref name=\"open\" /> Cortina, Livigno, and Predazzo are the other three places named for that ceremony.<ref name=\"open\" /> Coventry's title on the BBC page is IOC president.<ref name=\"open\" /> The \"first time\" sentence is the BBC's, and it is tied to her being the first woman in that office.<ref name=\"open\" /> Silver and bronze for the two leading countries are itemized on the BBC table.<ref name=\"bbcmed\" /> The Guardian's second-place line gives a gold count and a total.<ref name=\"gmed\" /> NBC is the page for the sport count, for the cross-country sweep under the NBC spelling, and for Cardona's gold in the sport the Winter Games had not held before.<ref name=\"nbc\" /> The Guardian medal table is the page for the other spelling of the skier and for the claim that the Norwegian gold count was a Winter record.<ref name=\"gmed\" />\n\nTwo cauldrons were lit. A woman opened the Games for the first time. Norway's 18 golds were a Winter record. Spain's one gold was its first in 54 years, in a sport the Winter Olympics had not held before.\n\n== References ==\n{{reflist}}\n\n[[Category:Olympics]]\n[[Category:True but improbable]]\n"
