        "daysAgo": 5,
        "comment": "expanded from GOV.UK, the 20 July speech, the BBC, Hansard, and the Guardian",
        "content": "{{true and funny|source=GOV.UK person page; speech of 20 July 2026; BBC, 20 July 2026; BBC profile updated 29 September 2026; Parallel Parliament, 2 September 2026; Guardian, 20 July 2026}}\n{{Infobox\n| title = Andy Burnham\n| office = Prime Minister\n| took office = 20 July 2026\n| labour leader = 16 July 2026\n| replaced = Sir Keir Starmer\n| how the BBC describes the leadership = without a contest\n| mayor = Greater Manchester\n| first mayoral election = May 2017\n| later mayoral elections = 2021 and 2024\n| mayoralty in the BBC report = from 2017 until the month before\n| culture secretary = heckled at the 20th-anniversary Hillsborough memorial\n| toll on the profile = 97 Liverpool fans killed in the 1989 crush\n| nickname on the profile = King of the North\n| chancellor he named = John Healey\n| chancellor replaced = Rachel Reeves\n| commons = 2 September 2026, answers as the Prime Minister\n}}\n\n'''Andy Burnham''' became Prime Minister on 20 July 2026.<ref name=\"gov\">GOV.UK, Andy Burnham. https://www.gov.uk/government/people/andy-burnham</ref> The same government page says he was elected Labour leader on 16 July of that year.<ref name=\"gov\" /> It records him as Mayor of Greater Manchester from an election in May 2017, with further elections in 2021 and in 2024.<ref name=\"gov\" />\n\n== The page that dates the offices ==\nGOV.UK puts the premiership, the leadership, and the mayoralty on one person page. The premiership date is 20 July 2026. The leadership date on that page is 16 July.<ref name=\"gov\" /> The mayoral line is older: elected in May 2017, then again in 2021, then again in 2024.<ref name=\"gov\" />\n\nThe page does not describe a general election. It dates a party office and a government office in the same month, and it dates a city-region office across three election years.<ref name=\"gov\" />\n\n== The speech ==\nHis first speech as Prime Minister, published the day the person page dates the office, opens with a place. The sentence is: \"I have just come from Buckingham Palace, where I have accepted His Majesty the King's invitation to form a government.\"<ref name=\"speech\">GOV.UK, first speech as Prime Minister, 20 July 2026. https://www.gov.uk/government/speeches/andy-burnhams-first-speech-as-prime-minister-20-july-2026</ref> He says he has just come from the palace. The invitation in that sentence is the King's. The task in that sentence is to form a government.<ref name=\"speech\" />\n\nThe speech and the person page share a date. One is a biography field. The other is the sentence he spoke.<ref name=\"gov\" /><ref name=\"speech\" />\n\n== Without a contest ==\nThe BBC, reporting on 20 July 2026, says he became Labour leader without a contest and that he replaced Sir Keir Starmer.<ref name=\"bbc20\">BBC News, 20 July 2026. https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/clyv9qe6wj7o</ref> The same report states what the office is. A UK prime minister is the leader of the largest Commons party.<ref name=\"bbc20\" /> The report also says he held the mayoralty from 2017 until the month before.<ref name=\"bbc20\" />\n\nThe government page's verb for the leadership is elected, and its date is 16 July 2026. The BBC's account of that week is that there was no contest, and that the predecessor was Sir Keir Starmer.<ref name=\"gov\" /><ref name=\"bbc20\" /> The BBC's definition of a UK prime minister is the leadership of the largest party in the Commons.<ref name=\"bbc20\" /> That definition is the report's, printed the day he took the office.\n\nThe mayoralty in the BBC sentence ends the month before the premiership. The elections the government page lists for that mayoralty are May 2017, then 2021, then 2024.<ref name=\"gov\" /><ref name=\"bbc20\" />\n\n== The memorial ==\nA BBC profile updated on 29 September 2026 says that, as culture secretary, he was heckled at the 20th-anniversary Hillsborough memorial.<ref name=\"profile\">BBC News profile, updated 29 September 2026. https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cp8pml5z8p6o</ref> The toll on that profile is 97 Liverpool fans killed in the 1989 crush.<ref name=\"profile\" /> The heckling led him to raise the issue in cabinet.<ref name=\"profile\" /> The profile says that raising the issue contributed to a second inquiry.<ref name=\"profile\" />\n\nThe sequence on the profile is the memorial, the cabinet, and the inquiry. The anniversary it names is the 20th. The year it gives the crush is 1989.<ref name=\"profile\" /> Those are the institutional facts on the page.\n\n== The name the profile uses ==\nThe same updated profile uses King of the North.<ref name=\"profile\" /> It says his Covid lockdown stand-off with the Conservative government helped earn him the nickname.<ref name=\"profile\" /> The stand-off named there is with the Conservative government. The nickname is the one the profile prints, and the profile attaches it to that stand-off.<ref name=\"profile\" />\n\nThe profile's update date is 29 September 2026. That is after the July dates on the government page. The heckling passage and the nickname passage sit on one profile. They describe different episodes.<ref name=\"profile\" />\n\n== The chamber ==\nOn 2 September 2026, Parallel Parliament's text of Hansard shows \"The Prime Minister (Andy Burnham)\" answering engagements.<ref name=\"hansard\">Parallel Parliament, Hansard, 2 September 2026, oral answers to questions. https://www.parallelparliament.co.uk/mp/andy-burnham/debate/2026-09-02/commons/commons-chamber/oral-answers-to-questions</ref> The office in the heading is Prime Minister. The name in parentheses is his. The business is oral answers.<ref name=\"hansard\" />\n\nThe sitting is in September. The speech that opened on the palace is dated in July.<ref name=\"speech\" /><ref name=\"hansard\" />\n\n== Up the street ==\nThe Guardian, on 20 July 2026, called his arrival a circuit breaker.<ref name=\"guard\">Guardian, 20 July 2026. https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2026/jul/20/andy-burnham-uk-prime-minister-meeting-king-charles</ref> He named John Healey as chancellor.<ref name=\"guard\" /> Healey replaced Rachel Reeves.<ref name=\"guard\" />\n\nThe count he gave that paper is one sentence: \"the sixth person in the last 10 years to walk up this street, the seventh prime minister since 2016.\"<ref name=\"guard\" /> The person doing the counting is him. The Guardian's own phrase for the arrival, separate from his sentence, is a circuit breaker.<ref name=\"guard\" />\n\nHe became prime minister without a general election. The speech started at Buckingham Palace. He counted himself the seventh prime minister since 2016.\n\n== References ==\n{{reflist}}\n\n[[Category:Politicians]]\n[[Category:True but improbable]]\n"
