        "content": "{{true and funny|source=ISU biography, stamped 21 September 2026; ISU women's results, 19 February 2026; BBC, 19 February 2026; Guardian, 19 February 2026; Guardian, 20 February 2026}}\n{{Infobox\n| title = Alysa Liu\n| born = 8 August 2005\n| birthplace on the ISU page = Clovis, California\n| birthplace on the 19 February Guardian = Clovis, California\n| birthplace on the 20 February Guardian = West Oakland\n| coaches = Phillip DiGuglielmo and Massimo Scali\n| world championship 2024/25 = place 1\n| olympics 2022 = place 6\n| olympics 2026 = place 1\n| team event = Olympic champion, 2026\n| total = 226.79 on 19 February 2026\n| short program = 3rd\n| free skate place = 1st\n| free skate score = 150.20, BBC\n| second = Kaori Sakamoto, 224.90\n| third = Ami Nakai, 219.16\n| national title = youngest US champion, at 13, in 2019\n}}\n\n'''Alysa Liu''' was born on 8 August 2005.<ref name=\"isu\">ISU biography, stamped 21 September 2026. http://results.isu.org/bios/isufs00103196.htm</ref> The ISU biography says Clovis, California.<ref name=\"isu\" /> Her coaches on that page are Phillip DiGuglielmo and Massimo Scali.<ref name=\"isu\" />\n\n== The sheet ==\nThe biography lists three places. The 2024/25 World Championship place is 1, and so is the 2026 Olympic Games place. The 2022 Olympic place is 6.<ref name=\"isu\" /> The line the biography prints about the gap comes next.<ref name=\"isu\" /> \"Liu retired from competing in 2022 and decided to come back in 2024.\"<ref name=\"isu\" /> The same page calls her Olympic champion in the 2026 team event.<ref name=\"isu\" /> It dates a personal best total of 226.79 to 19 February of that year.<ref name=\"isu\" />\n\nThe women's results page for those Games lists rank 1.<ref name=\"women\">ISU, Olympic women's final. http://www.isuresults.com/events/owg2026_Women.htm</ref> The points on that rank are the same total.<ref name=\"women\" /> The short program place on the sheet is 3rd. The free skate place is 1st.<ref name=\"women\" /> The sheet's timestamp is 19 February 2026, 22:47:51 UTC.<ref name=\"women\" />\n\nThe biography calls that total a personal best. The results page's 226.79 sits beside rank 1, with the short program in third and the free skate in first.<ref name=\"isu\" /><ref name=\"women\" /> The team-event line is a separate championship on the biography. The women's sheet is the individual final.<ref name=\"isu\" /><ref name=\"women\" />\n\n== The free skate ==\nThe BBC, on 19 February 2026, gives the free skate as 150.20 and the total as 226.79.<ref name=\"bbc\">BBC Sport, 19 February 2026. https://www.bbc.com/sport/articles/c33jz026mg3o</ref> The same report calls her the first American women's Olympic champion since Sarah Hughes in 2002.<ref name=\"bbc\" /> It says she had quit the sport aged 16 after missing out on a medal at Beijing 2022.<ref name=\"bbc\" /> The biography's place for that Olympics is 6, and the biography's own sentence is that she retired that year and came back in 2024.<ref name=\"isu\" /><ref name=\"bbc\" /> The Hughes comparison is the BBC's, and the year it names for Hughes is 2002.<ref name=\"bbc\" />\n\n== What she said on the ice ==\nThe Guardian, on 19 February 2026, quotes her as she left the ice: \"That's what I'm fucking talking about.\"<ref name=\"g19\">Guardian, 19 February 2026. https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/feb/19/alysa-liu-figure-skating-winter-olympics-2026</ref> The total in that story is 226.79.<ref name=\"g19\" /> Kaori Sakamoto's total in that story is 224.90.<ref name=\"g19\" /> Ami Nakai's total in that story is 219.16.<ref name=\"g19\" /> The Guardian that day says she is from Clovis, California.<ref name=\"g19\" /> It also says she was the youngest ever US national champion, at 13, in 2019.<ref name=\"g19\" />\n\nThe line as she left the ice is the one the Guardian printed, including the swear. The three totals are that paper's order: hers, then Sakamoto, then Nakai.<ref name=\"g19\" /> The national-champion sentence is the same day's paper, and the age it gives for that title is 13.<ref name=\"g19\" />\n\nThe next day's Guardian quotes a different sentence. \"My story is more important than anything to me, and that's what I will hold dear.\"<ref name=\"g20\">Guardian, 20 February 2026. https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/feb/20/alysa-liu-olympic-figure-skating-gold</ref>\n\n== Clovis, and West Oakland ==\nThe ISU biography says Clovis.<ref name=\"isu\" /> The Guardian of 19 February 2026 says Clovis.<ref name=\"g19\" /> The next day's Guardian says West Oakland.<ref name=\"g20\" /> This article does not pick a hometown.<ref name=\"isu\" /><ref name=\"g19\" /><ref name=\"g20\" />\n\nThe results sheet ranks her first. The free-skate score is the BBC's. The coaches are the biography's. The two towns are two papers, a day apart, and the federation's page agrees with the first of them.<ref name=\"women\" /><ref name=\"bbc\" /><ref name=\"isu\" /><ref name=\"g19\" /><ref name=\"g20\" />\n\nShe quit at 16. At 20 the total was 226.79. The skater in second scored 224.90. The gold was hers. One paper put her childhood in Clovis. The next day's paper put it in West Oakland.\n\n== References ==\n{{reflist}}\n\n[[Category:Sports]]\n[[Category:True but improbable]]\n"
