        "comment": "expanded from the Press Information Bureau, the Ministry of Home Affairs, the World Bank, and CNN",
        "content": "{{true and funny|source=Press Information Bureau, 14 August 2017, 25 November 2021, and 26 December 2024; Ministry of Home Affairs, Eighth Schedule; World Bank; CNN, 25 September 2014}}\n{{Infobox\n| title = India\n| free nation = 15 August 1947\n| constitution adopted = 26 November 1949\n| constitution in effect = 26 January 1950\n| at commencement = 395 Articles, 22 Parts, 8 Schedules\n| handwritten = 90,000 words\n| eighth schedule = 22 languages\n| population = 1.46 billion (2025)\n| electors 2024 = 97,97,51,847 registered Lok Sabha electors\n| votes polled = 64.64 crore\n| mars = Mangalyaan, $74 million\n| film noted = Gravity, $100 million\n}}\n\n'''India''' is the nation addressed by the President on 14 August 2017 with the sentence, \"On August 15, 1947, we became a free nation.\"<ref name=\"pib2017\">Press Information Bureau, President's address, 14 August 2017. https://www.pib.gov.in/newsite/PrintRelease.aspx?relid=169981</ref> A Press Information Bureau factsheet of 25 November 2021 says the Constitution was adopted on 26 November 1949, came into effect on 26 January 1950, and at commencement had 395 Articles, 22 Parts, and 8 Schedules. The handwritten original had 90,000 words.<ref name=\"pib2021\">Press Information Bureau factsheet, 25 November 2021. https://www.pib.gov.in/FactsheetDetails.aspx?Id=148584</ref>\n\n== 15 August 1947 ==\nThe address is dated 14 August 2017, the day before the anniversary the sentence names. 14 August is the day the address was given. 15 August 1947 is the day the sentence gives for becoming a free nation.<ref name=\"pib2017\" /> The factsheet on the Constitution is a different release. It does not restate that sentence.<ref name=\"pib2021\" />\n\n== At commencement ==\nThe factsheet gives two dates for the Constitution. It was adopted on 26 November 1949. It came into effect on 26 January 1950.<ref name=\"pib2021\" /> The counts it attaches to commencement are 395 Articles, 22 Parts, and 8 Schedules. The handwritten original had 90,000 words.<ref name=\"pib2021\" /> The word count is not a count of articles. The article count, the part count, and the schedule count are the other three figures on that factsheet.<ref name=\"pib2021\" />\n\n== A second 22 ==\nThe Ministry of Home Affairs text of the Eighth Schedule states that the Eighth Schedule consists of 22 languages.<ref name=\"mha\">Ministry of Home Affairs, Eighth Schedule. https://www.mha.gov.in/sites/default/files/EighthSchedule_19052017.pdf</ref> The 22 languages are that schedule. The 22 Parts are the factsheet's count of the Constitution at commencement.<ref name=\"mha\" /><ref name=\"pib2021\" /> They are the same numeral in two documents. They are not the same list. The factsheet's 8 Schedules are a count of schedules. The Eighth Schedule is one schedule, and the Home Ministry text says what that one schedule consists of.<ref name=\"pib2021\" /><ref name=\"mha\" />\n\n== The roll ==\nThe Press Information Bureau, on 26 December 2024, citing the Election Commission, reported 97,97,51,847 registered Lok Sabha electors in 2024, and 64.64 crore votes polled.<ref name=\"eci\">Press Information Bureau, 26 December 2024. https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleasePage.aspx?PRID=2088064</ref> The elector total is printed with that comma grouping. The votes are printed as crore, not as a second copy of the elector total.<ref name=\"eci\" /> The World Bank's population total for 2025 is 1.46 billion.<ref name=\"wb\">World Bank, population, India. https://data.worldbank.org/country/india</ref> The elector figure is registered Lok Sabha electors in 2024. The vote figure is votes polled. The population figure is the Bank's total. None of the three is a substitute for the others.<ref name=\"eci\" /><ref name=\"wb\" />\n\n== Seventy-four, and one hundred ==\nCNN, on 25 September 2014, reported that Mangalyaan cost $74 million, and that Prime Minister Modi noted that the budget of ''Gravity'' was $100 million.<ref name=\"cnn\">CNN, 25 September 2014. https://www.cnn.com/2014/09/25/news/india-mars-cost</ref> The mission figure and the film figure are the two numbers CNN puts in that relation.<ref name=\"cnn\" />\n\nThe mission to Mars cost $74 million. The prime minister pointed at a film that cost $100 million. The constitution, at the start, was 90,000 words in someone's handwriting.\n\n== References ==\n{{reflist}}\n\n[[Category:Countries]]\n[[Category:True but improbable]]\n"
