        "content": "{{true and funny|source=White House Historical Association; National Archives, inaugural address; Pulitzer Prizes, 1957; James N. Druckman, Journal of Politics, May 2003; Rice University address, 12 September 1962}}\n{{Infobox\n| title = John F. Kennedy\n| pt 109 = night of 1-2 August 1943\n| what struck it = a Japanese destroyer\n| crew = two died; eleven reached shore\n| decorations = Navy and Marine Corps Medal; Purple Heart\n| book = Profiles in Courage\n| prize = Pulitzer Prize, 1957, Harper\n| debate = the nation's first televised debate, against Richard Nixon\n| images = no valid evidence they played any role, Druckman 2003\n| houston = Rice University, 12 September 1962\n| moon line = We choose to go to the moon\n| inaugural = 20 January 1961\n| died = 22 November 1963\n}}\n\n'''John F. Kennedy''' commanded PT 109 on the night of 1-2 August 1943, when it was struck by a Japanese destroyer. Two of the crew died. Eleven reached shore. He received the Navy and Marine Corps Medal and a Purple Heart.<ref name=\"whha\">White House Historical Association, \"The Life and Presidency of John F. Kennedy\". https://www.whitehousehistory.org/the-life-and-presidency-of-john-f-kennedy</ref> On 20 January 1961 he said: \"And so, my fellow Americans: ask not what your country can do for you--ask what you can do for your country.\"<ref name=\"archives\">National Archives, President John F. Kennedy's inaugural address, 20 January 1961. https://www.archives.gov/milestone-documents/president-john-f-kennedys-inaugural-address</ref>\n\n== PT 109 ==\nThe White House Historical Association places the collision on the night of 1-2 August 1943. The boat was PT 109. It was under his command. The ship that struck it was a Japanese destroyer. The Association's count is two dead and eleven who reached shore.<ref name=\"whha\" /> The decorations it records for him from that service are the Navy and Marine Corps Medal and a Purple Heart.<ref name=\"whha\" /> The date is the night spanning 1 August and 2 August 1943.<ref name=\"whha\" />\n\n== Profiles in Courage ==\nThe Pulitzer Prizes for 1957 list ''Profiles in Courage'', by John F. Kennedy, published by Harper.<ref name=\"pulitzer\">Pulitzer Prizes, winners by year, 1957. https://www.pulitzer.org/prize-winners-by-year/1957</ref> The author on that list is John F. Kennedy. The publisher on that list is Harper.<ref name=\"pulitzer\" />\n\n== Houston ==\nAt Rice University, in Houston, on 12 September 1962, he said: \"We choose to go to the moon.\"<ref name=\"rice\">Address at Rice University, Houston, on the nation's space effort, 12 September 1962. https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/address-rice-university-houston-the-nations-space-effort</ref> The inaugural sentence is a different speech, on 20 January 1961, and the Archives text is the source for it.<ref name=\"archives\" /> The double hyphen is the Archives text. The address begins the sentence with \"And so, my fellow Americans.\"<ref name=\"archives\" /> One sentence chooses the moon. The other asks what the listener can do for the country.<ref name=\"rice\" /><ref name=\"archives\" />\n\n== The debate ==\nThe White House Historical Association says he faced Richard Nixon \"in the nation's first televised debate.\"<ref name=\"whha2\">White House Historical Association, on the televised debate and the end of the presidency. https://www.whitehousehistory.org/the-life-and-presidency-of-john-f-kennedy</ref> James N. Druckman, in the ''Journal of Politics'' for May 2003, wrote: \"there is no valid evidence that images played any role in the debate (Vancil and Pendell 1987).\"<ref name=\"druckman\">James N. Druckman, ''Journal of Politics'', May 2003. https://faculty.wcas.northwestern.edu/jnd260/pub/Druckman%20JOP%202003.pdf</ref> Druckman's sentence is about images. He points to Vancil and Pendell 1987. The radio story does not survive that sentence.<ref name=\"druckman\" />\n\n== 22 November 1963 ==\nThe White House Historical Association states that the presidency \"was cut short by an assassin's bullet on November 22, 1963.\"<ref name=\"whha2\" />\n\nThe radio story does not survive the paper. The inaugural sentence does.\n\n== References ==\n{{reflist}}\n\n[[Category:Presidents of the United States]]\n[[Category:True but improbable]]\n"
