        "content": "{{true and funny|source=BBC, 20 March 2026; Brown Daily Herald, 28 January 2006; Guardian, 22 March 2026; CNN, 20 March 2026; Los Angeles Times, 15 April 2001; Los Angeles Times, 2 October 1996}}\n{{Infobox\n| title = Chuck Norris\n| born = Carlos Ray Norris\n| birth on the Guardian = 10 March 1940\n| birth on the BBC = Oklahoma, 1940\n| died = 19 March 2026\n| age on the BBC = 86\n| cause = none stated\n| karate on the Guardian = six-time world middleweight champion, retired undefeated in 1974\n| film = The Way of the Dragon, 1972, fought Bruce Lee\n| series = Walker, Texas Ranger\n| role = Cordell Walker\n| network = CBS\n| years the BBC and CNN give = 1993 to 2001\n| seasons = nine on the Guardian, eight on CNN, eight and 203 episodes in the 2001 Los Angeles Times\n| facts site = a generator, launched 1 April 2005\n| hits = 48 million by 28 January 2006\n}}\n\n'''Chuck Norris''' died on the morning of 19 March 2026.<ref name=\"bbc\">BBC News, 20 March 2026. https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c0le8e5jg1xo</ref> The BBC, reporting the next day, says the family said he died yesterday, Thursday, morning.<ref name=\"bbc\" /> The age in that report is 86.<ref name=\"bbc\" /> The passing is described as sudden. No cause is given.<ref name=\"bbc\" />\n\n== The name and the title ==\nThe BBC says he was born Carlos Ray Norris in Oklahoma in 1940.<ref name=\"bbc\" /> The Guardian's obituary dates the birth to 10 March 1940, and the death to 19 March 2026.<ref name=\"guard\">Guardian obituary, 22 March 2026. https://www.theguardian.com/film/2026/mar/22/chuck-norris-obituary</ref> The BBC says he won karate championships in the 1960s.<ref name=\"bbc\" /> The Guardian calls him a six-time world middleweight karate champion who retired undefeated in 1974.<ref name=\"guard\" />\n\nThe BBC recalls that he was holding the world title when Bruce Lee cast him in ''The Way of the Dragon'' (1972), in which he fought Lee.<ref name=\"bbc\" /> The birth year is both papers' 1940. The day and the month are the Guardian's. The state is the BBC's.<ref name=\"bbc\" /><ref name=\"guard\" /> The championship wording differs by paper: the BBC says championships in the 1960s and a world title at the time of the casting, and the Guardian says six world middleweight titles and an undefeated retirement in 1974.<ref name=\"bbc\" /><ref name=\"guard\" />\n\nThe death date is the family's, as the BBC reported it on 20 March: yesterday, Thursday morning. The Guardian prints 19 March 2026.<ref name=\"bbc\" /><ref name=\"guard\" /> No page in these sources states a cause.<ref name=\"bbc\" />\n\n== Walker ==\nThe BBC says he played Cordell Walker on ''Walker, Texas Ranger'', on CBS, from 1993 to 2001.<ref name=\"bbc\" /> CNN, on 20 March 2026, gives the same years and names Cordell Walker.<ref name=\"cnn\">CNN, 20 March 2026. https://www.cnn.com/2026/03/20/entertainment/chuck-norris-death</ref> The same CNN page later says eight seasons.<ref name=\"cnn\" /> The Guardian says the show ran for nine seasons from 1993.<ref name=\"guard\" />\n\nThe Los Angeles Times, on 15 April 2001, is not an obituary. It says \"after eight seasons and 203 episodes.\"<ref name=\"lat01\">Los Angeles Times, 15 April 2001. https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-2001-apr-15-tv-52100-story.html</ref> The finale was slated for 12 May on CBS.<ref name=\"lat01\" /> He was 61.<ref name=\"lat01\" /> The show had premiered in April 1993.<ref name=\"lat01\" /> His line in that piece is: \"We wanted to quit while the show is still popular.\"<ref name=\"lat01\" />\n\nThe season counts do not match, and this article does not crown one. The Guardian's count is nine seasons. CNN's later line, on the page that already gave the years, is eight. The Times count from April 2001 is eight seasons and 203 episodes.<ref name=\"lat01\" /> The BBC gives the span and no season total.<ref name=\"bbc\" />\n\n== The belts ==\nThe Los Angeles Times, on 2 October 1996, says he had done martial arts since 1960, and that the first class was judo in South Korea.<ref name=\"lat96\">Los Angeles Times, 2 October 1996. https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-1996-10-02-ls-49498-story.html</ref> He went stateside, that piece says, with a black belt in tang soo do and a brown belt in judo.<ref name=\"lat96\" /> The sentence he gives the paper about the series is: \"I do 26 episodes of Walker every year.\"<ref name=\"lat96\" />\n\nThe 1996 piece is an interview during the run. The 2001 piece is the ending of the run, and the Times flags the episode count there. Neither is the 2026 obituary.<ref name=\"lat96\" /><ref name=\"lat01\" /><ref name=\"guard\" />\n\n== The generator ==\nThe Brown Daily Herald, on 28 January 2006, tells the origin of the facts. Ian Spector, Brown '09, saw a Something Awful thread of Vin Diesel facts.<ref name=\"brown\">Brown Daily Herald, 28 January 2006. https://www.browndailyherald.com/article/2006/01/the-spector-behind-those-randomly-generated-facts</ref> He launched a generator on 1 April 2005.<ref name=\"brown\" /> A poll followed, and Chuck Norris won it.<ref name=\"brown\" /> By the date of the Herald article, the Norris site had 48 million hits.<ref name=\"brown\" />\n\nThe thread Spector saw was about Vin Diesel. The poll is what attached the generator to Norris.<ref name=\"brown\" /> The hit count is the Herald's, for a site of generated facts, measured by 28 January 2006.<ref name=\"brown\" />\n\nThe website had 48 million hits. The facts on it were generated. The family said he died on a Thursday morning in March 2026. The obituary is not generated.\n\n== References ==\n{{reflist}}\n\n[[Category:Actors]]\n[[Category:True but improbable]]\n"
