        "content": "{{true and funny|source=England football profile; BBC, 12 August 2023; DFB data center; Bundesliga.com, 20 July 2026; Sky Sports, 24 March 2023; Guardian, 6 October 2026}}\n{{Infobox\n| title = Harry Kane\n| team = England captain\n| goals on the England page = 91\n| caps on the England page = 125\n| goals in the 6 October Guardian = 89, including four in that window\n| rooney record = 54th goal, 23 March 2023, against Italy\n| score on the England page = 2-0 up\n| score on Sky = a 2-1 win in Naples\n| 2018 World Cup = Golden Boot, six goals\n| debut = March 2015, Guardian\n| tottenham = academy from 2004\n| bayern fee = an initial 100 million euros, £86.4 million, plus add-ons\n| bayern contract = until 2027\n| trophies at departure = none, with club or country, BBC on 12 August 2023\n| german titles = two league titles, the 2025/26 cup, super cups in 2025 and 2026\n| bundesliga goals 2025/26 = 36\n}}\n\n'''Harry Kane''' is England captain.<ref name=\"eng\">England football, Harry Kane. https://www.englandfootball.com/england/mens-senior-team/squad/harry-kane</ref> The profile, which includes his October 2026 cap milestone, lists 91 senior goals and 125 caps.<ref name=\"eng\" /> It says he passed Wayne Rooney with his 54th goal, with England 2-0 up against Italy on 23 March 2023.<ref name=\"eng\" /> It lists a 2018 World Cup Golden Boot, with six goals.<ref name=\"eng\" />\n\n== Naples, or 2-0 ==\nSky Sports, on 24 March 2023, calls the record goal his 54th for England, and calls it a penalty.<ref name=\"sky\">Sky Sports, 24 March 2023. https://www.skysports.com/football/news/12841216/harry-kane-on-breaking-england-scoring-record-the-best-ive-felt-in-my-career</ref> Sky's result is a 2-1 win over Italy in Naples.<ref name=\"sky\" /> Sky says he was 29.<ref name=\"sky\" /> The line Sky quotes is: \"I'm feeling fit and strong — the best I've felt in my career.\"<ref name=\"sky\" />\n\nThe England page's score at the moment of the goal is 2-0 up, on 23 March 2023, against Italy, and Sky records the match as a 2-1 win in Naples.<ref name=\"eng\" /><ref name=\"sky\" /> Sky's detail for the goal is that it was a penalty.<ref name=\"eng\" /><ref name=\"sky\" /> This article prints both. It does not invent a later goal to join them.<ref name=\"eng\" /><ref name=\"sky\" /> The age Sky prints is 29. The quote about feeling fit is Sky's, from that day.<ref name=\"sky\" />\n\n== Ninety-one, and eighty-nine ==\nThe Guardian, on 6 October 2026, says the match against Czechia at Wembley was his 125th England appearance, level with Peter Shilton.<ref name=\"guard\">Guardian, 6 October 2026. https://www.theguardian.com/football/2026/oct/06/harry-kane-wins-record-equalling-125th-england-cap-peter-shilton</ref> The Guardian's goal sentence is its own: \"Kane has scored an England record 89 goals, including four in this window.\"<ref name=\"guard\" /> The debut in that piece is March 2015.<ref name=\"guard\" />\n\nThe England profile lists 91 senior goals and 125 caps, and it includes the October 2026 cap milestone.<ref name=\"eng\" /> The Guardian of 6 October that year lists 89 goals, including four in that window, and the same appearance count.<ref name=\"guard\" /> The two goal figures are different counts, dated on different pages. This article does not subtract one from the other.<ref name=\"eng\" /><ref name=\"guard\" /> The cap figure both pages are on, 125, is an appearance count on the Guardian and a cap count on the England profile. The opponent the Guardian names for that appearance is Czechia. The ground is Wembley.<ref name=\"guard\" />\n\n== Tottenham, then the fee ==\nThe BBC, on 12 August 2023, says Bayern signed him for an initial €100 million (£86.4 million) plus add-ons, on a deal until 2027.<ref name=\"bbc\">BBC Sport, 12 August 2023. https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/66484550</ref> At departure, the BBC says, he had never won a major trophy with club or country.<ref name=\"bbc\" /> He had been at Tottenham from the academy in 2004.<ref name=\"bbc\" />\n\nThe fee is initial, and the BBC prints both the euro figure and the sterling figure, plus add-ons. The contract end on that page is 2027. The trophy sentence is the state of play at departure, in a story dated 12 August 2023.<ref name=\"bbc\" /> The academy year is 2004.<ref name=\"bbc\" />\n\n== What the German pages list ==\nThe DFB data center lists Deutscher Meister for 2024/2025 and for 2025/2026.<ref name=\"dfb\">DFB data center, Harry Kane. https://datencenter.dfb.de/en/data-center/people/harry-kane/player</ref> The cup it lists is the DFB-Pokal.<ref name=\"dfb\" /> The super cup it lists is the Franz Beckenbauer Supercup, entered for 2025 and for 2026.<ref name=\"dfb\" />\n\nThe league site uses its own names for the cup and the super cup.<ref name=\"buli\">Bundesliga.com, 20 July 2026. https://www.bundesliga.com/en/bundesliga/news/harry-kane-2025-26-numbers-england-bauern-munich-goals-records-messi-38301</ref> Bundesliga.com, on 20 July 2026, says that in 2025/26 he won the Bundesliga, and that this was his second league title, plus the DFB Cup and the Franz Beckenbauer Supercup.<ref name=\"buli\" /> The Bundesliga goal total in that piece is 36.<ref name=\"buli\" />\n\nThe federation page's league titles are two seasons. Bundesliga.com calls the later one his second league title.<ref name=\"dfb\" /><ref name=\"buli\" /> The cup names differ by page: DFB-Pokal on the federation page, DFB Cup on the league site.<ref name=\"dfb\" /><ref name=\"buli\" /> The super cup's name on both pages is the Franz Beckenbauer Supercup, and the federation page lists it twice.<ref name=\"dfb\" /><ref name=\"buli\" /> The 36 is a Bundesliga goal count on the league's site, not an England count.<ref name=\"buli\" />\n\nHe left Tottenham without a major trophy. The German federation's page now lists two league titles, a cup, and two super cups. England's page lists 91 goals. A paper dated 6 October 2026 listed 89.\n\n== References ==\n{{reflist}}\n\n[[Category:Sports]]\n[[Category:True but improbable]]\n"
