        "comment": "expanded from the New Indian Express, The Hindu, the Indian Express, NDTV, and Deccan Herald",
        "content": "{{true and funny|source=New Indian Express, updated 16 March 2026; The Hindu, 5 May 2026; The Hindu, 10 May 2026; Indian Express, 5 May 2026; NDTV, 10 May 2026; Deccan Herald, 10 May 2026}}\n{{Infobox\n| title = 2026 Tamil Nadu Legislative Assembly election\n| polling = 23 April 2026, single phase\n| counting = 4 May 2026\n| voters = 5.67 crore\n| booths = 75,032\n| turnout = approximately 85.1 percent, Indian Express\n| seats = 234\n| tvk = 108\n| dmk = 59\n| aidmk = 46\n| majority = 108 is not a majority of 234\n| outgoing = M. K. Stalin\n| governor = Rajendra Vishwanath Arlekar\n| sworn in = C. Joseph Vijay, 10 May 2026\n| place = Jawaharlal Nehru Stadium, Chennai\n| age that day = 51\n| born = 22 June 1974\n| ministers sworn with him = nine\n| confidence vote = on or before 13 May 2026\n| oath = entire text at once, without paper\n| table = Perambur and Tiruchirappalli (East)\n}}\n\nThe '''2026 Tamil Nadu Legislative Assembly election''' was polled in a single phase on 23 April 2026.<ref name=\"nie\">New Indian Express, updated 16 March 2026. https://www.newindianexpress.com/states/tamil-nadu/2026/Mar/15/tn-assembly-elections-2026-polling-on-april-23-results-on-may-4</ref> The New Indian Express, in that update, says the ECI set counting for 4 May.<ref name=\"nie\" /> The process was to finish by 6 May, so that a government could take office by 10 May.<ref name=\"nie\" /> The voter roll in that report is 5.67 crore. The booth count is 75,032.<ref name=\"nie\" />\n\n== The count ==\nThe Hindu, on 5 May 2026, says TVK won 108 of 234 seats.<ref name=\"hindu5\">The Hindu, 5 May 2026. https://www.thehindu.com/elections/tamil-nadu-assembly/mk-stalin-resigns-as-tamil-nadu-chief-minister/article70941648.ece</ref> Governor Rajendra Vishwanath Arlekar accepted M. K. Stalin's resignation.<ref name=\"hindu5\" /> He asked Stalin to continue until alternate arrangements were made.<ref name=\"hindu5\" />\n\nThe Indian Express, the same day, says that as per ECI data TVK won 108 seats.<ref name=\"ie\">Indian Express, 5 May 2026. https://indianexpress.com/article/india/tamil-nadu-assembly-election-results-2026-winners-list-constituency-wise-seat-wise-10664508/</ref> The incumbent DMK won 59.<ref name=\"ie\" /> AIDMK won 46.<ref name=\"ie\" /> The Express dates polling to 23 April 2026.<ref name=\"ie\" /> Turnout, the Express says, was approximately 85.1 percent.<ref name=\"ie\" />\n\nThe New Indian Express had dated that polling day in March, as a single phase, with counting on 4 May. The Express results story uses the same polling date.<ref name=\"nie\" /><ref name=\"ie\" /> The Hindu's 108 of 234 is not a majority.<ref name=\"hindu5\" /> No source here describes a coalition. This article does not invent one.<ref name=\"hindu5\" /><ref name=\"ie\" />\n\nThe Express winners table lists C. Joseph Vijay for Perambur.<ref name=\"ie\" /> It also lists him for Tiruchirappalli (East).<ref name=\"ie\" /> The 108, the Express's presentation says, is a seat count.<ref name=\"ie\" /> This article records the two lines. It does not explain them away.<ref name=\"ie\" />\n\n== Until arrangements were made ==\nStalin's resignation was accepted on the count's reporting day. The governor's request, in The Hindu, was that he continue until alternate arrangements.<ref name=\"hindu5\" /> The Hindu's live coverage on 10 May 2026 says C. Joseph Vijay was sworn in as Chief Minister that day.<ref name=\"hindu10\">The Hindu, 10 May 2026. https://www.thehindu.com/elections/tamil-nadu-assembly/tamil-nadu-government-formation-tvk-swearing-in-vijay-oath-ceremony-new-chief-minister-live-updates-may-10-2026/article70960233.ece</ref>\n\nThe March schedule had said a government could take office by 10 May. The oath fell on that date.<ref name=\"nie\" /><ref name=\"hindu10\" /> Between those reports, The Hindu has Stalin asked to stay until arrangements existed.<ref name=\"hindu5\" /><ref name=\"hindu10\" />\n\n== The oath ==\nNDTV, on 10 May 2026, says C. Joseph Vijay, 51, was sworn in at Jawaharlal Nehru Stadium in Chennai.<ref name=\"ndtv\">NDTV, 10 May 2026. https://www.ndtv.com/india-news/vijay-takes-oath-as-tamil-nadu-chief-minister-with-9-tvk-leaders-thalapathy-is-tamil-nadu-chief-minister-11473780</ref> TVK, NDTV says, won 108 of 234.<ref name=\"ndtv\" /> Nine TVK ministers were sworn in with him.<ref name=\"ndtv\" /> A confidence vote was due on or before 13 May.<ref name=\"ndtv\" /> NDTV gives his date of birth as 22 June 1974.<ref name=\"ndtv\" />\n\nDeccan Herald, the same day, describes the oath. He said \"C Joseph Vijay ennum naan…\" and delivered the entire oath at once, without paper.<ref name=\"dh\">Deccan Herald, 10 May 2026. https://www.deccanherald.com/india/tamil-nadu/c-joseph-vijay-ennum-naan-tvk-president-assumes-office-as-tamil-nadu-chief-minister-3997934</ref> The opening words are the Herald's quotation. The manner is the Herald's: the whole text, in one delivery, with no paper.<ref name=\"dh\" />\n\nThe stadium and the age are NDTV's. The birth date is NDTV's. The ministers sworn alongside him are nine, and the confidence vote NDTV dates is on or before 13 May, after the oath of 10 May.<ref name=\"ndtv\" /> The Herald does not report the result of that vote. This article does not supply one.<ref name=\"dh\" /><ref name=\"ndtv\" />\n\nThe Hindu of 5 May has the seat total and the resignation. The Express of that day has the other two party totals it prints, DMK and AIDMK, the turnout, and the two constituencies beside one name.<ref name=\"hindu5\" /><ref name=\"ie\" /> The March piece has the calendar that put office-taking by 10 May, and the voter and booth figures.<ref name=\"nie\" />\n\nThe count was 4 May. The oath was 10 May, and he gave it without paper. A confidence vote was still ahead of him. The winners table had his name on two constituencies.\n\n== References ==\n{{reflist}}\n\n[[Category:Elections]]\n[[Category:True but improbable]]\n"
